xports, touched 172,952 units In the past quarter &quot;The turnaround strategy is de Ilvering results for us as is evi- dent in share gain in an intensely competitive market and impro ved profitability enabled by a slew of new product launches and customer-centric incentives,&quot; managing director Guenter But schek said in a news release. Jaguar Land Rover, meanwhile, is expecting an improved perfor mance in the ongoing quarter. JLR will soon be launching its first electric car and expects a &quot;stronger all-round performan- ce in the fourth quarter, driven by new models, seasonality and improved profitability,&quot; said Ralph Speth, the UK-based unit&#39;s chief executive. Consolidated finance costs on a consolidated level rose to 1,247 crore from 377 crore a year earli- er due to increased borrowings In both in India and at JLR. The com- pany&#39;s consolidated net debt stood at 47,777 crore primarily due to higher investments in JLR. ET Analysis +11 Get a dally mall with the most Important news on the automotive Industry from all media outlets. Send a blank mall to join@etauto.com to sign up.</p>', 0, '2026-01-24 04:07:06', '567', '706'),
(108, 109, 363, '<p>Hyundai Motor has unveiled the Crater Concept at the AutoMobility LA 2025. The model presents the concept of a compact off-road SUV designed to tackle tough terrain. The concept might go into production and is a preview of a future production model. It has been designed at the South Korean automaker&#39;s Hyundai America Technical Centre in Irvine, California. The Crater Concept features a compact monocoque structure. It is designed for versatility and can handle various terrains. The vehicle boasts steep approach and departure angles ideal for off-road adventures. It is equipped with 18-inch wheels and 33-inch off-road tires that provide excellent traction and ground clearance.</p>', 0, '2026-01-24 04:10:33', '533', '989'),
(109, 110, 364, '<p>March contract hovered around *169 per kg on Friday; Jan contract at 212 NCDEX Jeera Falls 20% on Rise in Guj Sowing Area Krishnakumar.PK @timesgroup.com Kochi: Jeera futures have fallen about 20% on the National Com- modity and Derivatives Ex- change (NCDEX) due largely to a sharp rise in the sowing area in Gujarat, the chief producer of the commodity, following a surge in prices last year. The March contract on Ncdex hovered around *169 per kg on Friday afternoon while the Ja- nuary contract, which is going for delivery, ruled around *212 per kg. The jeera futures reac- hed an all-time high of 223.60 per kg in December on the back of dwindling supply and robust export demand. But as exports slowed down, prices stabilised in the range of *210-212. The March and April contracts are currently trading around of 168-169 per kg, more than 40 less from current lev- els. &quot;Following high prices last year, the area under jeera has gone up 35% to 3.5 lakh hectares in Gujarat. Sufficient soil mois- ture in key growing regions ow- ing to normal monsoon is ex- pected to raise the production,&quot; said Ritesh Kumar Sahu, an analyst at Angel Commodities Broking. Jeera output is expect- ed to rise 25% next season. &quot;The expected production is between 5.5 lakh and 6 lakh tonnes, com- pared with 4.89 lakh tonnes in the last season,&quot; Sahu said. Jeera acreage in Saurashtra, particularly in Surendranagar and Morbi, increased over the year overtaking north Gujarat districts of Banaskantha and Patan due to the availability of Narmada canal irrigation. Prices are expected to stabilise Losing Earlier Gains *169 per kg Jeera March contract price output is on NCDEX on Friday expected *212 per kg January contract price on Friday *223.60 per kg All-time high price reached in December JEERA OUTPUT 5.5-6 lakh tonnes Expected in next season 4.89 lakh tonnes in last season to rise 25% next season 79,460 tonnes Jeera exports for six months ended September 30, 2017 (up 16% YoY) in the range of *165-170 per kg. Export demand, which has turned sluggish in anticipation of new crop, is likely to pick up by March, capping a further fall in prices. &quot;India will be the dom- inant supplier in the world mar- ket until the middle of the year when crop from Syria and Tur- key arrive,&quot; said Dipak Parikh, partner at Kanu Krishna Cor- poration, a major exporter. Un- like in previous years, the car- ryover stock has been limited to about 2 lakh bags (each bag of 55 kg), said Parikh. Vietnam is the largest impor- ter of Indian jeera, followed by Bangladesh and the UAE. Jeera exports for six months ended September 30, 2017, climbed 16% in volume and 20% in value from a year ago at 79,460 tonnes, worth 1,324.57 crore.</p>', 5, '2026-01-26 08:36:37', '1915', '800'),
(110, 111, NULL, '<h1>TCS, Nasdaq vying for NCDEX equity platform vendor deal</h1>\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"https://www.livemint.com/authors/ram-sahgal\"><strong>Ram Sahgal</strong></a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>3&nbsp;min read20 Dec 2025, 05:30 AM IST</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"logo\" src=\"https://www.livemint.com/lm-img/img/2025/12/19/600x338/NCDEX--621x414_1766160501195.jpg\" style=\"height:310px; width:550px\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p>NCDEX had launched operations in 2003, with commodity derivatives trading being reintroduced by the Vajpayee-led NDA government after a four-decade ban.<strong>(Mint)</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Summary</p>\r\n\r\n<p>NCDEX is reviewing technical proposals from top global vendors for an equity platform launching in FY27. It has raised&nbsp;₹770 crore for financing and aims to entice IPO-bound firms, navigating a competitive landscape dominated by established exchanges.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"gift article\" src=\"https://www.livemint.com/lm-img/subscription/images/gift_article/gift_icon.png\" style=\"height:20px; width:20px\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Gift this article</p>\r\n\r\n<p>India&#39;s only agri derivatives exchange National Commodity and Derivatives Exchange Ltd (NCDEX) is evaluating technical proposals from reputed global vendors&mdash;including its existing vendor LSEG (London Stock Exchange Group), US-based Nasdaq, London-headquartered Aquis and India&#39;s largest software exporter Tata Consultancy Services Ltd (TCS)&mdash;for its</p>', 0, '2026-01-26 08:38:26', NULL, NULL),
(111, 112, 365, '<p>NCDEX plans to make commodity derivatives a tool to address NPAs and volatile agri prices RAJESH BHAYANI Mumbai, 5 September V ijay Kumar V, managing director &amp; CEO, National details in futures or options from when pledged as mark to marker commodity exchange is making borrowers.&quot; Banks can ask their borrowers to hedge the price volatility risk of raw materials or commodities Commodity &amp; Derivatives against which they have taken Exchange Limited (NCDEX), loans in futures market, and in plans to make commodity deriva- options, if available. Even farmers tives, more importantly options, a can sell a part of their crop, esti- tool to address non-performing mated at the time of sowing, to assets (NPA) and volatile agricul- ensure they get the price they ture crop prices. intend to get even when it falls at the time of crop arrival. Farmers can buy put options of the crop they have sown. He told banks, market partici- pants and farmer organisations to use commodity options similar to an insurance policy and hedge their positions by locking mini- mum profits. NCDEX will propose to the reg- ulator to allow options in more agri commodities like chana, soyabean and castor seed, among others, as underlying futures in these com- modities are fairly liquid. It also proposes to make soya refined oil a deliverable contract and launch deliverable metals futures. The cost of buying option is minimal but if the price direction goes against what has been antic ipated, then the option will help in capping the profit and protect farmers from falling prices. Kumar said that in the first ever media interview after taking over as MD &amp; CEO in January that, &quot;finance and commodity markets are gradually converg- ing. Option market in commodi- ties is gaining momentum and farmer organisations can make use of this tool to lock minimum profit. Banks can also secure their lending risks by seeking hedge a risk is lower. This also help banks reduce asset turning non-per- forming. Options here works as an insurance policy. Anuj Gupta, head of commodi- ties and currencies at Angel Commodities, said, &quot;Usually, at the time of arrival, we generally wit- ness a sharp decline in prices and during scarcity of the commodity we noticed an appreciation in prices. During sharp movement in prices at the time of arrival and scarcity, one can use options. We have also noticed that few com- modity are still trading below its MSP like pulses, guarseed and oilseeds.&quot; He also said, &quot;At the time of arrival, if prices are going down, then farmers may use Put option of Even bank borrowers can buy put for the commodity which they have pledged to get a loan. The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has already told banks to ask their borrowers to hedge their com- modity price risk on commodity that particular commodity to pre- exchanges. Now, bank&#39;s com- vent it from the falling prices. It modity broking subsidiaries are just like an insurance of the prices taking membership of the comex- of the crop.&quot; However, to see that es. Kumar said, &quot;We have pro- happening, there is a need for an posed broking subsidiaries of enlarged list of commodities avail- banks suggesting them to make able for options in agri segment wider use of commodity futures &amp; and that should be liquid. option platform.&quot; For example, if a loan is taken of against guar seeds by a borrower of bank, he can buy put option guar seed from a bank broking sub- sidiary. If prices fall, he has to increase collateral for borrowing in general practice. But now, they have more efficient tool &#39;Options&#39;. So, when the price falls, put option premium goes up which can be adjusted for marking borrowing to market rate and he saves paying additional collateral. Bank can charge less collateral or reduce interest rate for such hedged stock is Commodity derivatives have other benefits too. Sanjay Kaul MD &amp; CEO - National Collateral Management Services, indicated that taking finance using com- modities as collateral will be ben- eficial to the borrower also if the same commodity is hedged in derivatives either in futures or in the option segment. He said, &quot;hedged contracts have minimal price risk and higher loan to value is safe for financiers also. Hence, lower collateral and lower margins are required in such cases.&quot; Even MCX, India&#39;s largest brass metal futures market friend- ly and will later introduce a few deliverable metal contracts. It is also expanding the delivery cen- tres for gold contract. At present, only Ahmedabad is the delivery centre and is adding New Delhi, Kolkata, Mumbai, Kochi, Chennai and Hyderabad to the list of deliv- ery centres. Even MCX has seen good trac- tion in its cotton futures contract with record 161,000 bales deliv- ered in the July contract which suggest good hedging. MCX is also helping farmers or their associa tion-bodies or farmers&#39; producers organisations by connecting them to convert Kapas (raw cotton) in cotton bales. 4019 BUY STOP SELL Currently, Sebi norms suggest WHAT NOW that when the option expires, if it ■Commodities hedged in has not been carried forward to the derivatives get easier finance next contract or not squared off, then the option devolves in futures and then all rules regarding futures are applicable, including margins to option holders as position becomes a futures position. However, Kumar said, &quot;We have proposed appropriate changes to the market regulator to make options more market friendly. This will help the option seller who is willing to give deliv- ery and smoothen the process. For giving delivery, he will comply with all norms and does not have to hold futures positions on option expiry and still wait for staggered delivery period. This way, the option becomes a virtu- al insurance policy and the option Farmers buying put options while planting can ensure better price when actual crop arrives NCDEX proposes to launch options in chana, soyabean and castor seed Plans to make soya refined Even commodity processors can sell put options. If price rise when they actually buy the pro- duce, price of the option that they have sold will give them profit and reduce actual pro- curement cost. The government has vested premium is like policy premium.&quot; the Sebi with powers to decide oil a deliverable and launch deliverable metals futures ■NCDEX has also sought futures in tur, urad ■MCX to increase delivery centers for gold ■MCX also considering metals contract with delivery based settlement listing and delisting commodities for derivatives from the list of 91 notified ones. Following this, NCDEX has proposed to the Sebi to relaunch tur and urad futures which were suspended in 2007. &quot;This makes pulses complex.com- prehensive for market players,&quot; said Kumar.</p>', 0, '2026-01-26 08:39:43', '873', '800'),
(112, 113, NULL, '<h1>MCX Q3 Results: Cons PAT jumps 151% YoY to Rs 401 crore, revenue soars 121%</h1>\r\n\r\n<p>SECTIONS</p>\r\n\r\n<p>MCX Q3 Results: Cons PAT jumps 151% YoY to Rs 401 crore, revenue soars 121%</p>\r\n\r\n<p>By&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/etreporter/author-shivendra-kumar-479260149.cms\" target=\"_blank\">Shivendra Kumar</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>,&nbsp;ETMarkets.comLast Updated: Jan 23, 2026, 09:12:00 PM IST</p>\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=economictimes\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Google Logo\" src=\"https://img.etimg.com/photo/124385886.cms\" style=\"height:30px; width:100px\" /></a></p>\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"https://www.whatsapp.com/channel/0029VaAr2nxHbFV91x3BKx10\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Whatsapp Follow Channel\" src=\"https://img.etimg.com/photo/104857408.cms\" style=\"height:18px; width:18px\" />Follow us</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Share</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Font Size</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Save</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Synopsis</p>\r\n\r\n<p>MCX posted a sharp Q3FY26 surge, with profit up 151% and revenue rising 121%, driven by booming derivatives volumes, strong bullion participation, new product launches and expanding options activity, supported by governance focus and readiness.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"MCX Q3 Results: Cons PAT jumps 151% YoY to Rs 401 crore, revenue soars 121%\" src=\"https://img.etimg.com/thumb/msid-127301574,width-210,height-158,imgsize-48184,resizemode-75/mcx-q3-results-cons-pat-jumps-151-yoy-to-rs-401-crore-revenue-soars-121.jpg\" style=\"height:158px; width:210px\" />ETMarkets.com</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Record volumes and new bullion products powered MCX&rsquo;s blockbuster quarter, lifting profits, revenues and EBITDA, while management highlighted governance, innovation and deeper market participation nationwide.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>India&#39;s largest commodity exchange, The Multi Commodity Exchange of India (MCX) reported a 151% jump in its consolidated net profit to Rs 401 crore in the quarter ended December 31, 2026 compared to Rs 160 crore in the year ago period. The profit after tax (PAT) is attributable to the owners of the company.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nThe revenue from operations in Q3FY26 stood at Rs 666 crore compared to Rs 301 crore in the corresponding quarter of the last financial year</p>', 0, '2026-01-26 08:42:20', NULL, NULL);
